-2018)","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Girma-Wolde-Giorgis_official_portrait.png/330px-Girma-Wolde-Giorgis_official_portrait.png","gender":"M","born":"1924-12-28","died":"2018-12-15","birthplace":"Addis Ababa","parties":[],"education":[],"occupations":["politician","economist","military personnel"],"wiki":"Girma Wolde-Giorgis"},"Q381071":{"id":"Q381071","name":"Hailemariam Desalegn","desc":"Prime Minister of Ethiopia from 2012 to 2018","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Hailemariam_Desalegn_-_Closing_Plenary-_Africa's_Next_Chapter_-_World_Economic_Forum_on_Africa_2011.jpg/330px-Hailemariam_Desalegn_-_Closing_Plenary-_Africa's_Next_Chapter_-_World_Economic_Forum_on_Africa_2011.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1965-07-19","died":null,"birthplace":"Southern Nations, Nationalities, and Peoples' Region","parties":["Southern Ethiopian People's Democratic Movement","Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front"],"education":["Addis Ababa University","Azusa Pacific University"],"occupations":["politician","minister"],"wiki":"Hailemariam Desalegn"},"Q15054736":{"id":"Q15054736","name":"Mulatu Teshome","desc":"former President of Ethiopia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Mulatu_Teshome_in_Warsaw_-_2018_(40792938585)_(cropped).jpg/330px-Mulatu_Teshome_in_Warsaw_-_2018_(40792938585)_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1957-01-29","died":null,"birthplace":"Arjo","parties":["Oromo Democratic Party","Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front"],"education":["Peking University"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician","minister"],"wiki":"Mulatu Teshome"},"Q50365049":{"id":"Q50365049","name":"Abiy Ahmed","desc":"Prime Minister of Ethiopia since 2018","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Prime_Minister_of_Ethiopia_Abiy_Ahmed_Ali_(cropped).jpg/330px-Prime_Minister_of_Ethiopia_Abiy_Ahmed_Ali_(cropped).jpg","gender":"M","born":"1976-08-15","died":null,"birthplace":"Beshasha","parties":["Oromo Democratic Party","Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front","Prosperity Party"],"education":["Addis Ababa University"],"occupations":["politician","military personnel","minister"],"wiki":"Abiy Ahmed"},"Q7399685":{"id":"Q7399685","name":"Sahle-Work Zewde","desc":"President of Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Sahle-Work_Zewde_in_2016.jpg/330px-Sahle-Work_Zewde_in_2016.jpg","gender":"F","born":"1950-02-21","died":null,"birthplace":"Addis Ababa","parties":["independent politician"],"education":["University of Montpellier","Lycée Guebre-Mariam"],"occupations":["politician","diplomat"],"wiki":"Sahle-Work Zewde"},"Q124831090":{"id":"Q124831090","name":"Taye Atske Selassie","desc":"Ethiopian politician and diplomat","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Taye_Atske_Selassie_MFA_Portrait.jpg/330px-Taye_Atske_Selassie_MFA_Portrait.jpg","gender":"M","born":"1956-01-13","died":null,"birthplace":"Gondar","parties":[],"education":["Addis Ababa University","University of Lancaster"],"occupations":["diplomat","politician"],"wiki":"Taye Atske Selassie"}},"summary":"Ethiopia, officially the Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia (FDRE), is a landlocked country located in the Horn of Africa region of East Africa. It shares borders with Eritrea to the north, Djibouti to the northeast, Somalia to the southeast, Kenya to the south, South Sudan to the west, and Sudan to the northwest. Ethiopia covers a land area of 1,104,300 km2 (426,400 sq mi). As of 2026, it has around 138 million inhabitants, making it the 10th-most populous country in the world and 2nd most populous country in Africa. The national capital and largest city, Addis Ababa, lies several kilometres west of the East African Rift that splits the country into the African and Somali tectonic plates.\nAnatomically modern humans emerged in what is now Ethiopia. In 980 BC, the Kingdom of D'mt extended its realm over Eritrea and the northern region of Ethiopia, while the Kingdom of Aksum maintained a unified civilisation in the region for 900 years. Christianity arrived to the kingdom in 330 AD, while Islam gained influence during the first Hijra in 615. After the collapse of Aksum in 960, the Zagwe dynasty ruled the north-central parts of Ethiopia. The Shewan usurper, Yekuno Amlak, overthrew the dynasty in 1270 at the Battle of Ansata, inaugurating the Ethiopian Empire and the Solomonic dynasty – asserting its lineage through Aksumite legitimacy by the biblical Solomon and Queen of Sheba, and Menelik I was the first emperor. Under Emperor Amda Seyon I (r. 1314–1344), the empire grew in prestige through territorial expansion against neighbouring Muslim territories. Later, the Ethiopian–Adal War (1529–1543) weakened the empire and opened the way for the Oromo expansion. The growing influence of the Oromo at the Gondarine court ultimately contributed to the fragmentation known as Zemene Mesafint, beginning in 1769. Emperor Tewodros II ended the Zemene Mesafint at the beginning of his reign in 1855, marking the reunification and modernisation of Ethiopia.\nIn the late 19th century, Ethiopia repelled foreign invasions from both Egypt and Italy, most notably defeating an Italian invasion at the Battle of Adwa in 1896. Under Emperor Menelik II, it also expanded its territory, remaining independent during the Scramble for Africa and establishing its current borders. Ethiopia lost its sovereignty in 1936, when Italy under Benito Mussolini invaded during the Second Italo-Ethiopian War, and following Ethiopia's defeat, Italian East Africa was established. In 1941, during World War II, it was liberated by the British Army, and its full sovereignty was restored in 1944. The Derg, a Soviet-backed military junta, took power in 1974 after deposing Emperor Haile Selassie and the Solomonic dynasty, and ruled the country for nearly 17 years amidst the Ethiopian Civil War. Following the dissolution of the Derg in 1991, the Ethiopian People's Revolutionary Democratic Front (EPRDF) dominated the country with a new constitution and ethnic-based federalism. Since then, Ethiopia has suffered from prolonged and unsolved inter-ethnic clashes and political instability marked by authoritarianism. From 2018, regional and ethnically based factions have carried out armed attacks in multiple ongoing wars throughout Ethiopia.\nEthiopia is a multi-ethnic state with more than 80 different ethnic groups. Christianity is the most widely professed faith in the country, with the largest denomination being the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, followed by Islam and a small percentage of traditional faiths. The country is a founding member of the UN, and hosts the headquarters of the African Union Commission and many other global non-governmental organisations focused on Africa, also becoming a full member of BRICS in 2024. Ethiopia is one of the least developed countries but is sometimes considered an emerging power, having the fastest economic growth in sub-Saharan African countries because of foreign direct investment in expansion of agricultural and manufacturing industries; agriculture is the country's largest economic sector, accounting for over 37% of the GDP as of 2022. Although the Ethiopian economy has experienced consistent growth, in terms of per capita income and the Human Development Index the country remains among the poorest in Africa. Ethiopia faces numerous challenges, including high rates of poverty, human rights violations, widespread ethnic discrimination, and a literacy rate of 60%.","history":"Ethiopia is one of the oldest countries in Africa; the emergence of Ethiopian civilization dates back thousands of years. Abyssinia or rather \"Ze Etiyopia\" was inhabited by Abyssinians (Habesha), specifically the Amhara\nand Tigrayans (speakers of Semitic languages) and the Cushitic, Oromo and Agaw. The Eastern escarpment of the Ethiopian highlands and the lowlands were the home of the various Muslim groups that formed the Ifat and Adal sultanates such as the Argobba, Afars, Harari/Harla and Somalis. In the central and south were found the ancient Sidama, Semitic Gurage and Omotic Wolaita, among others.\nOne of the first kingdoms to rise to power in the territory was the kingdom of Damot in the 10th century BC, which established its capital at Yeha. In the first century AD, the Aksumite Kingdom rose to power in the modern Tigray Region with its capital at Aksum and grew into a major power on the Red Sea, subjugating Meroe and its surrounding areas. In the early fourth century, during the reign of Ezana, Christianity was declared the state religion and not long after, the Aksumite empire fell into decline with the rise of Islam in the Arabian peninsula, which slowly shifted trade away from the Christian Aksum. It eventually became isolated, its economy slumped and Aksum's commercial domination of the region ended. The Aksumites gave way to the Zagwe dynasty, who established a new capital at Lalibela before giving way to the Solomonic dynasty in the 13th century. During the early Solomonic period, Ethiopia underwent military reforms and imperial expansion, allowing it to dominate the Horn of Africa.","historyTitle":"History of Ethiopia","curated":null,"indicators":{"population":[[1960,21376700],[1961,21906100],[1962,22465100],[1963,23051600],[1964,23666500],[1965,24310600],[1966,24952700],[1967,25620300],[1968,26337900],[1969,27072600],[1970,27829100],[1971,28600100],[1972,29374600],[1973,30166800],[1974,30957300],[1975,31723300],[1976,32534400],[1977,33167300],[1978,33734600],[1979,34238700],[1980,34428500],[1981,35304000],[1982,36701300],[1983,37740800],[1984,38735700],[1985,39842100],[1986,41037700],[1987,42460000],[1988,44150800],[1989,45879700],[1990,47609800],[1991,49717200],[1992,51853700],[1993,53708900],[1994,55611500],[1995,57537300],[1996,59467600],[1997,61404500],[1998,63363500],[1999,65